The Dirichlet-multinomial can be understood as taking draws from a multinomial distribution where each sample has a slightly different probability vector, which is itself drawn from a common Dirichlet distribution. In contrast with the multinomial distribution, which assumes that all observations arise from a single fixed probability vector. This enables the Dirichlet-multinomial to accommodate over-dispersed count data. Here we will discuss a community ecology example. Let’s assume we have observed counts of 
